#Storyboard

The teaser as a sequence of beats, written down as data before any of
it is drawn. Each beat has a name, a length, and the few words on screen
while it plays. There is no voice-over, so the captions carry the
argument, and they must stay short enough to read in the time they get.

The order follows the argument. It opens on the heritage -- literate
programming -- and on one real file, Petri Marking from pn-chomper. The
file's claims run for real; one of them is seen to mean something, as a
chomper splits in two. Then why this matters to an agent with a context
window, and last the whole project's name-graph, pulling back from the
one module to everything around it.

    @dataclass(frozen=True)
    class Beat:
        name: str
        duration: float
        caption: str

    BEATS = [
        Beat("heritage", 6.0, "Literate programming"),
        Beat("one-file", 5.0, "Prose and code in one context window"),
        Beat("claims-run", 4.5, "Every claim, checked"),
        Beat("split", 4.0, "Code that means what it says"),
        Beat("context", 4.2, "Less context, more meaning"),
        Beat("graph", 6.3, "The name graph"),
    ]

The last beat's caption gives way, at the very end, to the name of the
thing itself.

    END_CARD = "notlob"

##Timing

A beat starts where the one before it ends, so the timeline has no gaps
and no overlaps by construction.

    def timeline(beats: list) -> list:
        starts, clock = [], 0.0
        for beat in beats:
            starts.append((clock, beat))
            clock += beat.duration
        return starts

~example
    [start for start, _ in timeline(BEATS)][:3] == [0.0, 6.0, 11.0]

The whole teaser stays under thirty seconds, the length a timeline
autoplays without the viewer choosing to keep watching.

    LIMIT = 30.0

~example
    sum(beat.duration for beat in BEATS) <= LIMIT

##Captions

A caption is at most seven words, and every beat is named once, so a
scene can be found for each.

    CAPTION_WORDS = 7

    def beat(name: str) -> Beat:
        return next(b for b in BEATS if b.name == name)

~example
    all(len(b.caption.split()) <= CAPTION_WORDS for b in BEATS)
    len({b.name for b in BEATS}) == len(BEATS)
    beat("split").caption == "Code that means what it says"

